-- title:
--   Convergence radius $\mathcal R$ of the KM-myopic policy
-- statement:
--   Let $D_1,D_2,\dots$ be the demands, $Y^{KM}_t$ the orders of the KM-myopic policy with target ratio $r$ and initial order $y_1$, and $\tilde\psi=\tilde\psi(r)$ the upper $r$-quantile of the demand law. The **convergence radius** is
--   $$\mathcal R=\max\{0\le d\le\tilde\psi:\ P[Y^{KM}_t\ge d,\ \text{i.o.}]=1\},$$
--   the largest level up to $\tilde\psi$ that the policy reaches in infinitely many periods with probability one. The interval $[0,\mathcal R]$ is the **convergence interval**: Lemma 2 (i) of the source shows that the Kaplan–Meier estimators generated by the policy converge on it.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The maximum is `Nat.findGreatest` over $d\le\tilde\psi$ of the predicate "almost surely, $d\le Y^{KM}_n$ for infinitely many $n$". Orders are natural numbers, so $d=0$ always satisfies the predicate and the maximum exists; `Nat.findGreatest` returns exactly it.
-- source:
--   Huh, Levi, Rusmevichientong, Orlin, Adaptive Data-Driven Inventory Control with Censored Demand Based on Kaplan-Meier Estimator, Oper. Res. 59(4) (2011), doi:10.1287/opre.1100.0906, p. 935, Convergence Interval (definition of the radius R)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_KMNewsvendor_quantile
import Definitions.Def_KMNewsvendor_kmOrder

namespace KMNewsvendor

open MeasureTheory Filter

/-- The convergence radius `ℛ = max {0 ≤ d ≤ ψ̃* : P[Y_t^KM ≥ d, i.o.] = 1}` (p. 935) of the
KM-myopic policy with target ratio `r` and initial order `y₁`, for the demand process `D`
(`D 0` is the demand law). `d = 0` always qualifies, so `Nat.findGreatest` returns the maximum. -/
noncomputable def radius {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) (D : ℕ → Ω → ℕ)
    (r : ℝ) (y₁ : ℕ) : ℕ :=
  open Classical in
  Nat.findGreatest
    (fun d => ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, ∃ᶠ n in atTop, d ≤ kmOrder r y₁ (fun t => D t ω) n)
    (upperQuantile μ (D 0) r)

end KMNewsvendor


